}

delete {
  url: {{s3_endpoint}}/{{s3_bucket}}/{{chaos_marker_key}}
  body: none
  auth: awsv4
}

auth:awsv4 {
  accessKeyId: {{aws_access_key_id}}
  secretAccessKey: {{aws_secret_access_key}}
  sessionToken: 
  service: s3
  region: {{aws_region}}
  profileName: 
}

script:pre-request {
  const guards = require("./lib/guards.js");
  guards.markerBaselineGreen(bru);
}

tests {
  test("S3 deleted the object (HTTP 204)", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(204);
  });
  test("the request went to the guarded marker key", function () {
    expect(req.getUrl().endsWith("/" + bru.getVar("chaos_marker_key"))).to.equal(true);
    expect(bru.getVar("chaos_marker_key")).to.match(/\/_SUCCESS$/);
  });
}

docs {
  ## What this proves
  DESTRUCTIVE: removes the marker the Reporting pre-check waits on, as if extraction had not completed. The
  Parquet files and manifest are untouched. The matching restore is `D12 RESTORE put the S3 _SUCCESS marker
  back`; send it even if a request in between fails.

  Guarded. The pre-request script throws, and nothing is sent, unless `chaos_marker_key` is a captured
  `<prefix>/_SUCCESS` key AND `D5` proved the marker check green for that same key in this pass. Without
  the guard an uncaptured variable would be sent literally and S3 answers 204 for a key that never existed.

  ## Captures
  Nothing.

  ## Consumes
  - `chaos_marker_key` from `D2`
  - `chaos_marker_baseline_ok`, `chaos_marker_baseline_key` from `D5`

  ## When it fails
  "refusing to touch S3" or "refusing to delete": the guard stopped it; send `D1` to `D5` first and read why
  the baseline is not green. 403 on real AWS: the credential lacks `s3:DeleteObject`, which for a check
  credential is correct.
}
